questions.modelAnswer:
x = linspace(0, 4, 200);
y = exp(-x).*sin(5*x);
plot(x, y)
xlabel('x'), ylabel('y'), title('y = e^{-x} sin(5x)')

x is a 1 × 200 row vector. exp(-x) and sin(5*x) are both 1 × 200, because these functions act on each element. We want the product of corresponding values, so we use .*. Using * would ask for a matrix product of a 1 × 200 by a 1 × 200 matrix. The inner dimensions do not agree, so MATLAB would stop with an error. (In the title string, MATLAB's default TeX interpreter displays ^{-x} as a superscript.)

MATLAB version:
function [n, m] = countAbove(x, c)
    n = 0; total = 0;
    for k = 1:numel(x)
        if x(k) > c
            n = n + 1;
            total = total + x(k);
        end
    end
    if n > 0
        m = total/n;
    else
        m = NaN;
    end
end

The loop visits each element, counts those above c and adds them to a running total. The mean is total/n, with NaN when nothing qualifies (to avoid dividing 0 by 0). A vectorised alternative is mask = x > c; n = sum(mask); m = mean(x(mask)); which uses logical indexing. Note that mean of an empty array is NaN in MATLAB, so the empty case comes out right automatically.
